#!/bin/bash
set -euo pipefail

# =============================================================================
# baize-kube-help — Interactive guide to the baize-kube system
#
# Shows how to manage users, verify access, troubleshoot kubectl, use basic
# kubectl/minikube commands, and explains the system architecture.
# =============================================================================

cat <<'EOF'
================================================================================
                          baize-kube — USER GUIDE
================================================================================

baize-kube runs a rootless Kubernetes (minikube) cluster on this machine,
owned by a dedicated locked service user 'baize'. Human access is granted
through two Unix groups:

  baize-admins     full cluster admin (uses /etc/baize-kube/admin-kubeconfig)
  baize-consumers  limited per-user access (own ServiceAccount + kubeconfig)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. MANAGING USERS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Grant a user cluster access (creates their kubeconfig + ServiceAccount):
  sudo baize-kube-add-consumer <username>

Remove a user's access:
  sudo baize-kube-remove-consumer <username>

Refresh the shared admin kubeconfig (stale-port fix):
  sudo baize-kube-update-admin-kubeconfig

List provisioned users:
  sudo baize-kube-list-consumers

Rebuild a user's kubeconfig (e.g. after token rotation):
  sudo baize-kube-update-kubeconfig <username>

Make a user a full admin:
  sudo usermod -aG baize-admins <username>
  (user must log out and back in for the group to take effect)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. CHECKING A USER'S ACCESS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Check which group a user is in (id prints uid/gid and ALL group names):
  id <username>

As an admin, verify a consumer's permissions inside the cluster
(auth can-i lists what the kubeconfig is ALLOWED to do — the safest way
to answer "can user X do Y?"):
  KUBECONFIG=~<username>/.kube/config kubectl auth can-i --list
  KUBECONFIG=~<username>/.kube/config kubectl get pods

Admins use the shared admin kubeconfig (set automatically at login via
/etc/profile.d/baize-kube.sh when a member of baize-admins):
  kubectl get nodes

Test that everything works end-to-end with a one-shot busybox pod.
Flag-by-flag: --image picks the container image; --rm DELETES the pod
when it exits; -it attaches a terminal (interactive + tty); 
--restart=Never means "run once, never restart" (required with --rm);
everything after -- is the command the pod runs. The pod is created,
prints OK, and is automatically deleted:
  kubectl run baize-test --image=busybox:latest \
      --rm -it --restart=Never -- echo OK

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. KUBECTL NOT WORKING? (TROUBLESHOOTING)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
EOF

cat <<'EOF'
Symptom: "The connection to the server ... was refused"
  The cluster is not running. Start it:
    sudo -u baize env XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/$(id -u baize) \
        systemctl --user start minikube
  Or check its status:
    sudo -u baize env XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/$(id -u baize) \
        systemctl --user status minikube

Symptom: "error: no configuration has been provided"
  Your KUBECONFIG is not set. Admins: log out/in so /etc/profile.d/baize-kube.sh
  applies, or run:
    export KUBECONFIG=/etc/baize-kube/admin-kubeconfig
  Consumers: ensure ~/.kube/config exists (run baize-kube-add-consumer if not).

Symptom: "connection refused 127.0.0.1:<port>" although the cluster is up
  The admin kubeconfig is a snapshot taken at install time. Its port (a
  random podman port-forward) changes whenever the minikube container is
  recreated. Regenerate it:
    sudo baize-kube-update-admin-kubeconfig
  (then refresh consumers: sudo baize-kube-update-kubeconfig <username>)

  NOTE: the API endpoint is LOCAL to this machine (127.0.0.1). Remote
  admins need an SSH tunnel or to run kubectl on the Pi itself.

Symptom: "connection refused" right after a reboot
  The minikube service may still be starting (it boots a podman machine
  first). Wait ~1-2 minutes, then retry. If it stays down:
    sudo -u baize env XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/$(id -u baize) \
        podman machine ls        # machine should be 'Running'
    sudo -u baize env XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/$(id -u baize) \
        journalctl --user -u minikube

Regenerate the SHARED admin kubeconfig (after cluster recreation):
  sudo baize-kube-update-admin-kubeconfig

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. BASIC KUBECTL COMMANDS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(-A is shorthand for --all-namespaces; -f follows output like tail -f;
 -it allocates an interactive terminal; -n <ns> targets one namespace)

  kubectl get nodes                     list cluster nodes
  kubectl get pods -A                   list all pods, all namespaces
  kubectl get services -A               list all services
  kubectl describe pod <name>           detailed pod info (debugging)
  kubectl logs <pod-name>               print a pod's logs
  kubectl logs -f <pod-name>            follow a pod's logs live
  kubectl exec -it <pod> -- sh         open a shell inside a pod
  kubectl apply -f <file.yaml>          create/update resources from YAML
  kubectl delete pod <name>            delete a pod
  kubectl run test --image=busybox:latest --rm -it --restart=Never -- echo hi
                                        one-shot test pod (auto-deleted)
  kubectl auth can-i --list            what can MY kubeconfig do?

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. BASIC MINIKUBE COMMANDS (run as baize, with its environment)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

All minikube operations must run as the baize user, which needs two
environment variables: XDG_RUNTIME_DIR (where systemd per-user sockets
live) and CONTAINER_HOST (where the podman machine API socket is).
Rather than typing them every time, define a helper function:

  bk() { sudo -u baize env \
      XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/$(id -u baize) \
      CONTAINER_HOST=unix:///run/user/$(id -u baize)/podman/minikube-api.sock \
      "$@"; }

(Paste it into your ~/.bashrc to keep it. "$@" forwards every argument
you give bk to the command it runs.)

Then:
  bk minikube status                    cluster status
  bk minikube dashboard                 open the web dashboard
  bk minikube logs                      dump cluster logs for debugging
  bk minikube ssh                       shell into the cluster node
  bk minikube pause / unpause           pause/resume (keeps resources)
  bk minikube update-context             fix kubeconfig after IP changes
  bk podman machine ls                  VM state (should be 'Running')

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. HOW THE SYSTEM IS SET UP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  baize user      Locked service account (no interactive login). Owns the
                  cluster, the podman machine, and all cluster state under
                  /home/baize/.minikube. Member of kvm group for QEMU
                  hardware acceleration; has subuid/subgid ranges for
                  rootless container user-namespace mapping.

  Podman machine  A QEMU virtual machine 'minikube' (default 4 CPU / 6144 MB
                  memory / 30 GB disk) owned by baize. minikube's podman
                  driver creates the Kubernetes node as containers INSIDE
                  this machine. The machine's size can be set BEFORE
                  installation with environment variables:
                    sudo env BAIZE_KUBE_MACHINE_CPUS=2 \
                             BAIZE_KUBE_MACHINE_MEMORY=4096 dpkg -i ...
                  (minimums: 2 CPU, 4096 MB — the node plus ~1 GB guest OS). The podman client reaches
                  the machine via the API socket:
                    unix:///run/user/<baize-uid>/podman/minikube-api.sock
                  (exported as CONTAINER_HOST everywhere it matters).

  Boot chain       systemd user@<uid>.service (lingering enabled)
                    -> minikube.service (oneshot, RemainAfterExit)
                       -> ExecStartPre: podman machine start minikube
                       -> ExecStart: minikube start --driver=podman \
                          --container-runtime=containerd

  Access model    Admins:    shared admin kubeconfig (full RBAC power)
                  Consumers: per-user ServiceAccount (consumer-<username>)
                             + token Secret + kubeconfig at
                             ~<username>/.kube/config

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. INTERNALS (DEEPER DETAILS)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Cgroups          The kernel boots with cgroup_memory=1 cgroup_enable=memory
                   (patched into /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt by the installer).
                   systemd delegates cpu/cpuset/io/memory/pids controllers to
                   the baize user session via
                   /etc/systemd/system/user@.service.d/delegate.conf — required
                   for rootless containers to set resource limits.

  Podman helpers   The podman machine stack needs two helper binaries that
                   Debian ships outside podman's search path. The installer
                   links them into /usr/libexec/podman/:
                     gvproxy    (user-mode networking for the VM)   <- /usr/bin/gvproxy
                     virtiofsd  (host<->VM filesystem sharing)      <- /usr/libexec/virtiofsd
                   Without the virtiofsd link, machine start fails.

  QEMU/KVM         The Raspberry Pi 5 exposes /dev/kvm. baize is in the kvm
                   group so the machine uses hardware virtualisation. Without
                   it QEMU falls back to software emulation (TCG) — orders of
                   magnitude slower.

  Rootless model   Nothing runs as root. minikube -> podman (rootless, as
                   baize) -> podman machine VM (QEMU as baize) -> containerd
                   inside the machine -> Kubernetes node containers.

  Cluster state    /home/baize/.minikube/           minikube profiles
                   /home/baize/.config/containers/  podman machine config
                   /home/baize/.local/share/containers/  machine disk image
                   /etc/baize-kube/                  admin kubeconfig, consumers.conf

  Removal          On apt remove/purge: minikube cluster deleted, podman
                   machine stopped and removed, baize user and its state
                   deleted. cmdline.txt is left patched (harmless; may be
                   needed by other software).

Full documentation: /usr/share/doc/baize-kube/
================================================================================
EOF